WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.320
Speaker 1: Welcome everybody, I want to start&nbsp;
today off and start the week off with something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:10.000
we really haven't done in about in about six&nbsp;
weeks, which is just kind of go over what's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:14.480
going on with COVID because things have changed&nbsp;
pretty dramatically over the last six weeks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:20.000
We've not done the weekly COVID updates that&nbsp;
at one point we were doing. My thought was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:24.080
first of all, with so much else going on.&nbsp;
There's no shortage of things to talk about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:31.280
but also let's really give this Omicron wave four&nbsp;
to six weeks to burn through and see where we are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:38.880
once we close in on mid-February. And for the most&nbsp;
part, in the United States, things are looking so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:44.880
much better that there are now what I would call&nbsp;
serious discussions about moving into the endemic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:50.080
phase. Whereas before there was a lot of kind of&nbsp;
hear no evil, see, no evil. Let's just go back to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:55.760
pretending everything is normal. Things are not&nbsp;
normal now, but the discussions about next steps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.760 --> 00:01:01.040
make a lot more sense. And as I've said before,&nbsp;
we before we look at the numbers, if there is not.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:11.200
A more deadly new variant. There is sort of&nbsp;
nothing else to wait for before we figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:16.960
out what will what will things be like with&nbsp;
this as an endemic thing? Now endemic doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:22.560
necessarily mean no risk, and to many people, it&nbsp;
still means a risk. But the question is sort of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:30.480
if not now when? Because if we are moving into an&nbsp;
endemic phase with no really serious new variant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:36.400
for one year, two years, three years, what are we&nbsp;
going to do over those one, two or three years? If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:41.120
not now? What would be different in two years if&nbsp;
the situation remains sort of as it is now now?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:45.520
One caveat to that is even though cases are&nbsp;
dropping dramatically, as I will soon show you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:50.240
it's going to take some time for the&nbsp;
hospitals to empty out. We are still seeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:57.360
hospitals quite full right now because&nbsp;
of the waves peak now many weeks ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:04.000
and it will take some time for that to abate,&nbsp;
assuming there's no new, really contagious and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:09.680
or deadly variant. But let's look at where we&nbsp;
are right now. If you look at the United States,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:16.720
daily cases are now down from an insane peak&nbsp;
of 800000 cases a day, all the way down to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:22.080
two hundred and thirty nine thousand now. That's&nbsp;
still a lot. It puts a sort of at the Delta Peak,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:29.360
but with a variant with significantly lower rate&nbsp;
of hospitalization rate. Remember, that's down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:35.760
about through almost almost three quarters. You&nbsp;
look at some individual states, you look at New&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:43.040
York. New York peaked at 75000 cases a day and&nbsp;
is down to about 8000, you know, almost a 90&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:49.040
90 percent reduction. You look at Massachusetts.&nbsp;
Massachusetts peaked at about 24000 cases a day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:55.840
down all the way down to 3000. OK, that's a&nbsp;
massive, massive reduction. Even places like Texas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:03.120
seeing a reduction only about 50 percent, but&nbsp;
still going in the downward direction. And then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:09.120
when you look globally, globally, the decline&nbsp;
is earlier. We are earlier in the decline.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:16.160
We had gotten to 3.3 million global cases a day&nbsp;
now down to 2.7 million. So there are parts of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:23.120
the world that are nowhere near doing well, as we&nbsp;
would say. And we will talk about some of those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:31.600
in a moment. But the important thing for our&nbsp;
purposes is that we are seeing countries, states,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:38.640
school districts start to talk about what are the&nbsp;
next steps now in terms of vaccinating kids under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:44.400
five. There's the hope. I mean, I think the most&nbsp;
optimistic prediction is by March that may start.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:50.880
It's plausible. It's not going to be March&nbsp;
and it could be April, maybe or maybe even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:56.320
May. But you are starting to see a lot of&nbsp;
places make decisions about travel. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.320 --> 00:04:02.560
Australia, which has been one of the tightest&nbsp;
strictest places since the start of the pandemic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:07.040
will be reopening borders to international&nbsp;
travel, as reported by the BBC.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:15.280
That's from this morning. So Australia starting&nbsp;
to open. There are a number of additional European&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:20.400
countries that are moving towards what we might&nbsp;
call an exit from the pandemic. Italy, Switzerland&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:26.720
and Finland are going to be easing restrictions&nbsp;
and guidelines. Germany and Belgium so far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:35.360
are not. But many other countries, Denmark and&nbsp;
others are going back to normal, as we might say.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:40.400
Still, of course, with the caveat that there&nbsp;
are going to be people who are going to continue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:44.000
doing whatever they've been doing.&nbsp;
And one of the interesting things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:49.600
that we've talked about before is there's this&nbsp;
discussion of when can we treat this like flu?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:56.160
One answer to make it an empirical answer and&nbsp;
not an emotional answer. One answer would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:03.200
when the death rate matches that of flu, that that&nbsp;
would be something to look at. It is hypothesized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:11.360
that the AMA coren overall death rate is about 0.2&nbsp;
percent. Flu is zero point one five. So we're not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:19.360
there yet, but it's going in that direction. One&nbsp;
additional caveat to that is the death rate for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:25.200
the vaccinated is significantly lower than the&nbsp;
zero point two percent. The death rate for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:33.680
unvaccinated is significantly higher. So what you&nbsp;
can safely. The framework that you can accurately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:38.880
assess for yourself does depend significantly&nbsp;
on whether you are or aren't vaccinated. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:43.280
one of those questions is well, when the death&nbsp;
rate becomes that of the flu, it would make sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:48.080
to go back to normal, right? Well, no. The reality&nbsp;
is that these two years have exposed for a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:54.400
of people. I wasn't as safe as I thought during&nbsp;
flu season, and there are absolutely people who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.200 --> 00:06:01.760
used to not take any precautions when flu&nbsp;
was rampant during flu season. But even.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:06.640
If and when COVID risk goes back down to match&nbsp;
flu, it will still feel more dangerous and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:10.880
people will be more aware of that risk and they&nbsp;
may continue to take precautions. And of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:16.000
everybody should do exactly what makes sense&nbsp;
for them. In conversation with doctors and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:20.960
based on their risk assessment. But that's the&nbsp;
conversation that's happening. South Korea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:28.960
as reported by the Korea Herald, pushes to treat&nbsp;
COVID 19 like flu. They have lots of cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:35.200
but low severity, relatively low hospitalizations&nbsp;
relative quite quite a low death rate. So that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:42.080
the South Korean perspective. In New Jersey, the&nbsp;
governor will be ending the school mask mandate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:48.880
next month in March, and there are many&nbsp;
municipalities I saw some in. And by the way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:52.080
there are places that never did anything. It's&nbsp;
like, I'm not even talking or thinking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:56.560
those I'm talking about. Places that did stuff&nbsp;
are now having conversations. There are some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.560 --> 00:07:02.080
municipalities in Massachusetts that are moving&nbsp;
away from the masks. Some in New York. A number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:08.080
different places. Caveat things are still not good&nbsp;
in a lot of places. Russia has hit a new record.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:12.960
They currently have 10 times more cases than a&nbsp;
month ago. To the extent that we can believe the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:17.920
Russian data, it is now 10 times higher cases&nbsp;
than a month ago. That's not good for them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:27.040
Hong Kong is bracing for more curbs or guidelines&nbsp;
mandates as cases are doubling every three days.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.040 --> 00:07:35.520
Very complicated situation in Hong Kong, China&nbsp;
is locking down Bays City on the Vietnam border&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:40.960
as cases are continuing to go up. Still low&nbsp;
numbers. And of course, China has taken a sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:47.120
at least officially a zero COVID approach now,&nbsp;
whether that's zero COVID or zero COVID reporting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:53.840
You know, there's debate about that, but there are&nbsp;
significant lockdowns happening there as well. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.840 --> 00:08:00.400
what can we do? Well, I think again, the question&nbsp;
is reminder we've been here before. What is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:07.760
different now? The difference is there is more&nbsp;
support for the discussion now about moving into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.760 --> 00:08:11.840
the endemic phase in terms of public opinion&nbsp;
than there ever was before. But there's also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:16.480
more support from medical experts. We spoke&nbsp;
to Vincent Racaniello two weeks ago. We've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.480 --> 00:08:23.680
read the opinions of many other public health&nbsp;
experts and if things change, it will require&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:29.920
a different approach. But if we are going in this&nbsp;
direction, then there is this question of what are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:34.080
the milestones we are waiting for? And there are&nbsp;
people who are going to keep themselves in a sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:39.840
of purgatory for a very long time. That's the&nbsp;
reality. And every individual has to balance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:47.680
You know, their their risk tolerance, their health&nbsp;
situation and the mental health impact that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:52.800
that is undeniable. I mean, you know, the right is&nbsp;
not wrong when they say there is a mental health&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:59.600
impact of doing this. It's a question of when does&nbsp;
the balance shift? And the answer is different for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:03.920
everybody. So we're going to continue&nbsp;
monitoring all of these decisions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.480 --> 00:09:11.920
and seeing if the down trend continues, which&nbsp;
if it does within another two weeks, we could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:18.880
we could be 95 to 97 percent down in terms&nbsp;
of daily cases. And when you have that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:23.440
combined with the effectiveness of vaccines at&nbsp;
preventing death, you really have a dramatically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:28.560
different situation to consider in terms&nbsp;
of what every individual wants to be doing.

